virtio-pci: suspend-to-idle keeps the devices running

The emulator's suspend-to-RAM is suspend-to-idle (mem_sleep_default=s2idle; QEMU's arm64 virt
machine has no PSCI SYSTEM_SUSPEND): the VM keeps running and only the vCPUs idle, so a virtio
device keeps its state, as one with PCI PM's No_Soft_Reset does. QEMU sets that bit only for a
device behind a PCIe port, and ours sit on the root bus with no PM capability, so the driver would
reset each device at suspend and restore it at resume. The 9p shares and virtio-gpu have no
restore and stayed dead after a resume (an ls of /host hung in D state), and the remote's port,
reset, could not wake the guest. Suspend and resume leave the devices alone; freeze/thaw and
poweroff/restore, hibernation's, still reset them.

--- a/drivers/virtio/virtio_pci_common.c
+++ b/drivers/virtio/virtio_pci_common.c
@@ -626,31 +626,20 @@
 	return virtio_device_restore(&vp_dev->vdev);
 }
 
-static bool vp_supports_pm_no_reset(struct device *dev)
-{
-	struct pci_dev *pci_dev = to_pci_dev(dev);
-	u16 pmcsr;
-
-	if (!pci_dev->pm_cap)
-		return false;
-
-	pci_read_config_word(pci_dev, pci_dev->pm_cap + PCI_PM_CTRL, &pmcsr);
-	if (PCI_POSSIBLE_ERROR(pmcsr)) {
-		dev_err(dev, "Unable to query pmcsr");
-		return false;
-	}
-
-	return pmcsr & PCI_PM_CTRL_NO_SOFT_RESET;
-}
-
+/*
+ * The emulator: suspend-to-RAM is suspend-to-idle under QEMU's virt machine, which keeps every
+ * device running while the vCPUs idle, as a device with No_Soft_Reset does. The devices sit on
+ * the root bus, which gives them no PM capability to say so, and a reset would lose the 9p shares
+ * and the GPU (their drivers have no restore) and keep the remote's port from waking the guest.
+ */
 static int virtio_pci_suspend(struct device *dev)
 {
-	return vp_supports_pm_no_reset(dev) ? 0 : virtio_pci_freeze(dev);
+	return 0;
 }
 
 static int virtio_pci_resume(struct device *dev)
 {
-	return vp_supports_pm_no_reset(dev) ? 0 : virtio_pci_restore(dev);
+	return 0;
 }
 
 static const struct dev_pm_ops virtio_pci_pm_ops = {
